<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="practice-improper-integrals">

  <title>Improper Integrals</title>

  <exercise xml:id="practice-improper-integrals-1">
    <title>Convergence and Divergence</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Determine whether each improper integral converges or diverges.
        If an integral converges, determine its value.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the convergence of
          <m>
            \int_0^2 \frac{2x}{x^2-4}\,dx.
          </m>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The integrand is unbounded at <m>x=2</m>, so write
        </p>

        <me>
          \int_0^2\frac{2x}{x^2-4}\,dx
          =
          \lim_{b\to2^-}
          \int_0^b\frac{2x}{x^2-4}\,dx.
        </me>

        <p>
          Since an antiderivative is
          <m>\ln|x^2-4|</m>,
        </p>

        <me>
          \lim_{b\to2^-}
          \left(
            \ln|b^2-4|-\ln 4
          \right)
          =
          -\infty.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore, the improper integral diverges.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the convergence of
          <m>
            \int_0^\infty xe^{-x^2/2}\,dx.
          </m>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>u=x^2/2</m>, so <m>du=x\,dx</m>. Then
        </p>

        <me>
          \int_0^\infty xe^{-x^2/2}\,dx
          =
          \int_0^\infty e^{-u}\,du
          =
          1.
        </me>

        <p>
          Hence the integral converges to <m>1</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="practice-improper-integrals-2">
    <title>Epidemic Model</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        The rate at which people get sick during an epidemic of the flu
        can be approximated by
      </p>

      <me>
        r(t)=1000te^{-0.5t},
      </me>

      <p>
        where <m>r(t)</m> is measured in people per day and <m>t</m> is
        measured in days since the start of the epidemic.
        How many people get sick throughout the entire epidemic?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The total number of people who become sick is
      </p>

      <me>
        \int_0^\infty1000te^{-0.5t}\,dt.
      </me>

      <p>
        Integration by parts gives
      </p>

      <me>
        \int_0^\infty te^{-0.5t}\,dt=4.
      </me>

      <p>
        Therefore,
      </p>

      <me>
        \int_0^\infty r(t)\,dt
        =
        1000(4)
        =
        4000.
      </me>

      <p>
        Thus, the model predicts that <m>4000</m> people become sick.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="practice-improper-integrals-3">
    <title>Photon Detection</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        After a short laser pulse, the rate at which photons are detected
        by a sensor is modeled by
      </p>

      <me>
        r(t)=\frac{500}{(1+t)^2},
      </me>

      <p>
        where <m>r(t)</m> is measured in photons per microsecond and
        <m>t</m> is measured in microseconds since the pulse.
        How many total photons are detected over all time?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The total number of detected photons is
      </p>

      <me>
        500\int_0^\infty(1+t)^{-2}\,dt.
      </me>

      <p>
        Hence,
      </p>

      <me>
        500
        \lim_{b\to\infty}
        \left[
          -\frac{1}{1+t}
        \right]_0^b
        =
        500.
      </me>

      <p>
        Therefore, a total of <m>500</m> photons are detected.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

</section>